[{:db/id 17592186119931 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-03fcc7d2-8c99-53e1-b5b5-d7a0f5cd296f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-03fcc7d2-8c99-53e1-b5b5-d7a0f5cd296f" :question/id #uuid "03fcc7d2-8c99-53e1-b5b5-d7a0f5cd296f" :question/math-academy-id "q-42481" :question/problem "Given that $det(A)=14$, and $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}(3I)=-13$\n- $-\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(I)=-13$\n- $\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=-28$" :question/worked-solution "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})=\\text{det}(A)=14\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(I)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3I & =3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a diagonal matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & ={3}^{3}=27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}(3I)=14-27=-13\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n-\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(I)=-14+1=-13\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=14-14=0\\ne -28\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-11a0d3a7-d6ab-5c8b-9be2-dd9dc9a08b2a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-11a0d3a7-d6ab-5c8b-9be2-dd9dc9a08b2a" :answer-field/id #uuid "11a0d3a7-d6ab-5c8b-9be2-dd9dc9a08b2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a2457563-b0a9-5e93-b34f-725229856d7b" :answ